12. (Calmet) — In this last part, Jeremias foretells what would befall the Jews in Egypt, and other nations. (Worthington)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","You, people of Juda. (Haydock) — This pagan acknowledges that God had punished his people, (C. xviii. 16.; Calmet) as Titus did at the last siege. Josephus. (Haydock) — He acknowledges the justice of God. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Eyes, with all kindness. C. xxxix. 12. (Haydock) — Before thee. Thou hast liberty to choose. Gen. xiii. 9. and xx. 15. and xxiv. 51."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","And. Hebrew \"And as he (the people, or Godolias) will not return, dwell;\" or, \"He had not yet answered, when the general said: Return to Godolias.\" He perceived that the prophet was not desirous of going to Babylon; and he insinuated that such a step would not be for his advantage, as the captives would not return. — Presents. He had been plundered like the rest."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Masphath; because it was near Jerusalem, and an ancient place of devotion. (Calmet)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Captains, who had fled from Sedecias, and effected their escape. Jos. Ant. x. 11."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","With you. They might naturally suspect Godolias, and think that the Chaldees would seize them as soon as they should know of their escape."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Answer. Hebrew \"stand before,\" as minister (Haydock) for the Chaldees. You need not be jealous of my power; and I can answer that they will not hurt you, provided you continue quiet. (Calmet) — He promises to act in behalf of the people, as a governor ought to do. (Worthington)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Countries. They had fled to avoid the taxes, &c. but found no redress. Lam. i. 3."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Came, having discovered the design of Ismahel, whose ambition prompted him to murder the governor, that he might reign: as he was of the royal family. C. xli. 1. (Calmet)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Baalis. Queen. (Grotius) — The Ammonites sought the entire ruin of the Jews."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Do not. He did right in forbidding this assassination, which could never be authorized, even to prevent a similar crime. Godolias should have taken precautions: but he was too open, (Calmet) like Eumenes. (Grotius)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}